===Alternative forms===
* {{alter|en|camboose}}
* {{alter|en|cambouse}}

===Etymology===
From {{der|en|fr|cambuse}}, from {{der|en|dum|kabuys}}, {{m|dum|kabuis}} (Modern {{cog|nl|kombuis|t=galley; kitchen}}).

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/kəˈbuːs/}}
* {{audio|en|En-au-caboose.ogg|Audio (AU)}}
* {{rhymes|en|uːs|s=2}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|obsolete|nautical}} A small [[galley]] or [[cookhouse]] on the [[deck]] of a small [[vessel]].
# {{lb|en|historic|nautical}} A small [[sand]]-filled container used as an [[oven]] on board ship.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1821|title=Narrative of the Most Extraordinary and Distressing Shipwreck of the Whale-Ship Essex …|author=Owen Chase|page=|passage=On the second day out, while sailing moderately on our course in the Gulf Stream, a sudden squall of wind struck the ship from the SW. and knocked her completely on her beam-ends, stove one of our boats, entirely destroyed two others, and threw down the '''cambouse'''. }}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1841|title=Journal of the Franklin Institute |author=|page=113 |ISBN=|passage=This stove is to be made in the form of a Franklin, but is to be furnished with an oven, and other means of cooking; its appearance is therefore more like that of the old fashioned '''caboose''', than of a Franklin stove. }}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1881|title=The Story of an Earnest Life|author=Eliza Davies |page=226 |ISBN=|passage=A tremendous billow, fringed with foam, swept over our deck, carrying the cook's '''caboose''', cooking utensils and stove right overboard into the sea.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2002|title=Cayman Islands|author=Don Philpott |page= |ISBN=|passage=The kitchens were kept separate because cooking was done in a '''caboose''', a wooden box filled with sand and heated by a wood fire.}}
# {{lb|en|US|rail transport}} The last [[car]] on a [[freight]] [[train]], having [[cooking]] and [[sleeping]] [[facilities]] for the [[crew]]; a [[guard]]’s [[van]].
#: {{syn|en|guard's van|q1=obsolete}}
# {{lb|en|slang|baby-talk|or|euphemistic}} [[buttocks]]
# {{lb|en|slang|sports}} The person or team in last place.
# {{lb|en|informal|often|in combination}} A youngest child who is born after a long gap in time.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1987 |title=Something's Wrong with My Child!|author=Harriet Wallace Rose |page= |ISBN=|passage=Jimmy was seven and had just finished first grade, so that made Nancy our '''caboose''' baby — our bonus child — our swan song.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1987 |title=Growing Child Research Review - Volumes 5-7|author=|page= |ISBN=|passage="'''Caboose'''" children, the late-born last offspring in the family, didn't suffer from this as much.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2007 |title=Baby Changes Everything|author=Beth K. Vogt |page=145 |ISBN=|passage=After looking back on her own experience, she thought of some ways parents could help ease the transition for their '''caboose''' kid.}}

